Kafka concepts:
a. Topic: a specific stream of data, like a table of database
b. Partition: a topic has several partitions located in different brokers, message inside partition is consecutive
c. Broker: server, each partition has a leader broker, and the other brokers are followers
d. Consumer group: a collection of consumers to read data from partition
e. Producer: Producer sends events/messages(header, key, payload)
f. Offset: a unique index for message, so we know what message is consumed
g. Zookeeper: manage brokers

1. Given N (number of partitions) and M (number of consumers,) what will happen when N>=M and N<M
   respectively?
if N >= M, a consumer may be responsible for more than 1 partition. Otherwise, some consumer may be idle. Because
a partition can only be consumed by one consumer.

2. Explain how brokers work with topics?
Topics contains several partitions that located in different broker. A partition has a leader broker which reads
and writes. Other brokers are followers, replicas. If the leader broker is down, one of the other followers will
become the new leader.

3. Are messages pushed to consumers or consumers pull messages from topics?
Consumer read messages from topics

4. How to avoid duplicate consumption of messages?
First, messages are consecutive inside a partition. And we have Offset. Each message has a unique id, offset.
When consumer commits a message, the offset is recorded.

5. What will happen if some consumers are down in a consumer group? Will data loss occur? Why?
If some consumers are down, other consumers will take over the partition. The data won't loss because we have
offset record, so we know which one is consumers and where we should start from the next one.

6. What will happen if an entire consumer group is down? Will data loss occur? Why?
If the entire consumer group is down, the data is still stored in partition. No data loss, because we have retain
time for data.

7. Explain consumer lag and how to resolve it?
Consumer lag is that consumer doesn't process data timely, so some data remain in partition waiting to be processed.
We can add more partition and more consumer to scale it up.

8. Explain how Kafka tracks message delivery?
Using offsets. Offsets are stored in a special internal topic: consumer_offsets. It records which offset is consumed.

9. Compare Kafka vs RabbitMQ, compare messaging frameworks vs MySql (Why Kafka)?
Kafka: For event-driven architecture, big data pipelines, or analytics.
RabbitMQ: For work queues, short tasks, and request-response systems.
Messaging Framework: If your system needs real-time data movement, event processing, or service decoupling.
MySQL: If you need structured data storage, transactions, and complex querying.

